WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.430
INTRO:
Remember Toshiba?

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:05.770
In the 2000s, they were one of the most popular
laptop brands in the world.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:11.770
In 2007, they controlled 7.3% of the global
laptop market which is more than Apple’s

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:13.590
laptop market share in 2018.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:19.030
Aside from dominating the laptop space, Toshiba
was a massive player within the hard drive/memory

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.450
space and consumer electronics.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.420
And let’s not forget their famous Times
Square advertisement.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.480
But, over the past 10 years, they’ve fallen
off a cliff.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.329
Their revenues have halved, their net income
has stabilized at marginal numbers, they sold

00:00:33.329 --> 00:00:36.989
off a lot of their business, they were caught
for accounting fraud, they shut down their

00:00:36.989 --> 00:00:41.109
laptop business, one of their subsidiaries
filed for bankruptcy, and they have overall

00:00:41.109 --> 00:00:43.420
just been struggling to stay afloat.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.469
Somehow, within just 15 years, Toshiba went
from being a highly respected Japanese electronics

00:00:48.469 --> 00:00:50.989
company to mostly being forgotten about.

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:55.799
So, here’s how Toshiba got so large in the
first place, where did went wrong, and why

00:00:55.799 --> 00:00:58.579
this may be the end of Toshiba.

00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:04.010
PRE TOSHIBA:
Unlike most tech companies today, Toshiba

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:08.270
wasn’t founded within the past few decades
or even the past hundred years.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:11.680
Toshiba was actually founded way back on July
11, 1875.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.710
A few years before the company was founded,
the ministry of engineering commissioned a

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.640
Japanese engineer named Tanaka Hisashige to
develop telegraphic equipment.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:24.450
At the time, the phone was yet to be invented,
but telegraphs had been around for nearly

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.630
40 years.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:30.460
However, Japan had yet to really embrace the
technology, so this commission was basically

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:32.759
a part of the country’s modernization effort.

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:37.789
Anyway, along the development process, Tanaka
established the company Tanaka Seisakusho

00:01:37.789 --> 00:01:42.869
which means Tanaka Engineering Works and this
is what would eventually become Toshiba.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:47.550
Initially, the company was just selling telegraph
equipment, electric switches, and various

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.119
communications equipment.

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:53.590
After Tanaka passed away in 1881, his son
took over the company and sold a part of the

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:55.390
company to General Electric.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:59.359
This allowed them to expand into the production
of torpedoes and mines which was wanted by

00:01:59.359 --> 00:02:00.780
the Japanese Navy.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.869
However, once the navy figured out how to
produce their own weapons, they ditched Tanaka

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:08.640
Engineering which drove the company to insolvency.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.990
By 1893, the company ran out of money, and
Mitsui bank took over the company and renamed

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.300
it Shibaura Engineering Works.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:19.990
Over the next several years, they developed
some utilitarian products such as a waterwheel-powered

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:22.330
turbine generator and a radio transmitter.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:26.110
But, none of these really caught steam and
made the company that much money.

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:29.440
So, in 1910, the company formed another deal
with GE.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.500
GE agreed to share its technology with Shibaura
in exchange for a quarter of the company.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.950
After this deal, Shibaura finally started
to make some progress within heavy electrical

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:41.560
machinery, but then they got beat down quite
hard.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.700
In 1923, Japan experienced the Great Kanto
Earthquake which claimed the lives of 100,000

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.170
people, many of which were Shibaura employees.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:53.500
And just as Shibaura finished reconstruction,
the Japanese government banned the production

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.230
of home appliances in order to conserve materials
for World War 2.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:02.060
To make things worse, the US and Japan weren’t
exactly friends throughout the war, so the

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.630
partnership with GE was iffy to say the least.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:09.680
So, in 1939, Shibaura decided to merge with
another GE-powered Japanese company called

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.750
Tokyo Denki, and together they formed Tokyo
Shibaura Denki.

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:18.510
Unlike Shibaura who focused on heavy machinery
and appliances, Tokyo Denki was mainly focused

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.450
on small consumer electronics.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:24.280
Throughout the rest of the war, the duo tried
to supply the military with radios, vacuum

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.790
tubes, and generators, but this led to their
factories being constantly bombed.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:32.590
Nonetheless, these government contracts left
them much better off than most engineering

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.290
companies which got decimated during the war.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:39.150
And through the 1940s and 50s, these guys
picked up all of the devastated engineering

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.790
companies and formed a super conglomerate.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:46.260
And while these guys were struggling on their
own, together, they were about to become an

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.400
unstoppable force.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.170
TOSHIBA’S HAYDAY:
Over the next few decades, Tokyo Shibaura

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:55.210
churned out product after product.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:04:00.510
First, they had the TAC Digital Computer in
1954, then there was the transistor TV and

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:06.070
the microwave oven in 1959, and finally, there
was the color video phone, the MRI system,

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:08.500
and the laptop throughout the 70s and 80s.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.580
Aside from launching a slew of products, Toshiba
also launched a slew of companies including

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:17.330
Toshiba Music, Toshiba Chemical, Toshiba Lighting
and Technology, and Toshiba America Information

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:18.330
Systems.

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:21.850
When many of these subsidiaries were formed
though, the company wasn’t actually called

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:22.850
Toshiba.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:26.980
It was still called Tokyo Shibaura Denki,
and it wasn’t till 1984 that they combined

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:31.890
the words Tokyo and Shibaura to create Toshiba
and made it the company’s official name.

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.980
It wasn’t all smooth sailing though.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:38.650
In 1987, one of Toshiba’s subsidiaries,
Tocibai, Machine, was caught selling submarine

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.860
propeller production equipment to the Soviet
Union.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:46.000
And this was in violation of the CoCom agreement
which established an embargo on certain countries.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.390
This incident hurt Japanese American relations
and resulted in the prosecution of two senior

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:51.390
executives.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:55.910
But, despite this setback, Toshiba continued
to march forward into the new century.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:05:00.520
In 2001, Toshiba formed a partnership with
Orion Electric who was one of the world’s

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.330
largest OEM manufacturers.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:07.060
Orion helped Toshiba bring TVs and other video-related
products to the North American market.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:11.021
This partnership was going well enough that
Toshiba actually stopped in-house CRT television

00:05:11.021 --> 00:05:12.590
production in 2004.

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:16.970
Shortly after, they also shut down in-house
plasma television production.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:19.640
But this partnership didn’t last forever.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:23.430
The contract only specified a 7-year partnership
and the two companies went their separate

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:25.130
ways in 2008.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:29.639
Unfortunately, this was basically the peak
of Toshiba’s business, but they didn’t

00:05:29.639 --> 00:05:31.389
know it at the time.

00:05:31.389 --> 00:05:35.180
Toshiba was actually celebrating their success
and they bought the famous Time Square display

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:37.440
from Discover Card in 2007.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:42.260
For the next 11 years, Toshiba displayed the
countdown every New Year, but really, they

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:44.820
were just counting down to their own demise.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:50.050
A NUCLEAR DISASTER:
Probably one of Toshiba’s biggest blunders

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:52.810
was investing heavily in nuclear energy.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:57.270
Toshiba had a presence within nuclear energy
production since 1970, but their biggest bet

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.490
didn’t come till 2006.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:04.770
Toshiba spent $5.4 billion to acquire a majority
stake in the nuclear power plant company Westinghouse

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:05.770
Electric.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:08.760
At the time, this was seen as an extremely
desirable acquisition.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.540
It was clear that coal and oil weren’t good
long-term energy solutions.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:16.490
Meanwhile, renewable sources like hydropower
and solar couldn’t produce nearly enough

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:17.490
energy.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:21.230
So, it seemed like safe nuclear power production
was the future.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:25.180
Toshiba actually bid 3 times more than what
analysts thought Westinghouse was worth to

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:30.090
acquire their stake, and after the acquisition,
Toshiba’s CEO Atsutoshi Nishida proudly

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:33.580
announced that nuclear power was going to
grow 50% by 2020.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:38.310
But, in reality, Nuclear power production
hasn’t really grown since 2000.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:45.050
Aside from the market itself not really growing,
Toshiba also experienced some bad luck.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:48.010
In 2011, an earthquake/tsunami disabled the
power supply and cooling of three Fukushima

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:49.620
Daiichi reactors.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:54.139
This led to all three cores melting within
the next few days and causing a nuclear accident.

00:06:54.139 --> 00:06:57.410
This was the last thing Japan wanted to happen.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:00.460
19,500 people had died due to the earthquake
and tsunami.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:05.110
And now, they had to evacuate 100,000 people
to minimize radiation.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:09.010
As you would guess, Japan was done with nuclear
energy after this incident.

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:12.790
They stopped investments into the industry
and shut down all nuclear reactors which led

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:15.070
to many countries following suit.

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:19.990
Despite all this, Toshiba’s CEO at the time,
Norio Sasaki, assured investors that nuclear

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:22.560
energy would continue to be a strong option
moving forward.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.360
But, he wouldn’t be able to keep up this
charade for much longer.

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:30.230
One of Westinghouse’s biggest customers
Chicago Bridge &amp; Iron Company closed its nuclear

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:31.230
division.

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:35.260
In an effort to keep the contracts alive,
Westinghouse bought their nuclear division,

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:39.169
but it quickly became clear that these contracts
could not be completed profitably.

00:07:39.169 --> 00:07:43.380
At one point, it became so bad that keeping
the nuclear division open would bankrupt Toshiba

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:44.380
itself.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:49.010
So, in March of 2017, Toshiba let Westinghouse
file for bankruptcy.

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:52.720
And that was basically the end of Toshiba’s
nuclear business.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:57.560
CORE DECLINE:
Toshiba may have lost big on their nuclear

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:00.880
bet, but they still had their core electronics
business right?

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.500
Well, not really.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:06.900
You see, Toshiba had spent so much capital
and effort in the nuclear business that they

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:09.050
let their core business slip away from them.

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:13.120
Throughout the late 2000s and early 2010s,
we witnessed the mobile revolution which Toshiba

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:14.669
completely missed out on.

00:08:14.669 --> 00:08:18.560
And as for their laptop business, consumers
were slowly forgetting about Toshiba.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.850
Here’s the thing, most people never bought
Toshiba laptops because they loved Toshiba.

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:28.370
Most people just bought Toshiba laptops cause
they were practical, affordable, and durable.

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:32.659
In other words, Toshiba didn’t have much
brand loyalty and the laptop industry was

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:34.999
becoming increasingly unprofitable.

00:08:34.999 --> 00:08:38.589
Survivors like Dell and HP completely altered
their strategy.

00:08:38.589 --> 00:08:42.529
They more or less accepted 0% net margins
on their consumer hardware and focused on

00:08:42.529 --> 00:08:44.540
trying to make money through alternative methods.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:48.640
Toshiba, however, didn’t get the memo and
they got squeezed as laptop profit margins

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:49.860
trended towards 0.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:54.369
They held on as long as they could, but in
August of 2020, they announced that they would

00:08:54.369 --> 00:08:56.119
exit the laptop industry after 35 years.

00:08:56.119 --> 00:09:01.259
In the meantime, their television business
wasn’t fairing too well either.

00:09:01.259 --> 00:09:06.259
In the early 2010s, Toshiba had fully embraced
3D televisions which were super hot at the

00:09:06.259 --> 00:09:07.259
time.

00:09:07.259 --> 00:09:10.490
But, it didn’t take long for consumers to
write it off as a gimmick and move back to

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:11.890
regular TVs.

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:15.529
Around the same time, Toshiba also lost the
war against Blu-ray.

00:09:15.529 --> 00:09:19.830
Toshiba was the principal supporter of the
HD DVD format, but as studios increasingly

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:23.199
supported Blu-ray, Toshiba had no choice but
to give in.

00:09:23.199 --> 00:09:27.490
Honestly though, given that DVDs themselves
are becoming obsolete, the loss of this business

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:30.910
was inevitable even if they had won against
Blu-ray.

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:35.459
With Toshiba’s laptop business gone, and
their TV business on a massive decline, Toshiba

00:09:35.459 --> 00:09:38.300
only had a few things left to hold onto.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:44.089
TOSHIBA TODAY:
Today, Toshiba is no doubt just a shell of

00:09:44.089 --> 00:09:45.120
their former self.

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:48.420
Currently, they’re staying afloat through
two main sectors which include the memory

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.160
business and the industrial equipment business.

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:55.449
Toshiba was actually the pioneer of flash
memory back in the 1980s, but they’ve lost

00:09:55.449 --> 00:09:58.439
significant market share to Samsung over the
past decade.

00:09:58.439 --> 00:10:02.129
To make things even worse, Toshiba sold their
flash memory unit to save the rest of their

00:10:02.129 --> 00:10:03.910
business in 2018.

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:08.680
They did get a solid $18 billion from the
sale, but honestly, I think they gave up their

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:11.009
last source of hope with this sale.

00:10:11.009 --> 00:10:16.110
After all, the entire company of Toshiba itself
is only worth $19 billion now.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:19.680
Considering all these hardships, I don’t
think you’d be too surprised to hear that

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:25.069
Toshiba’s executives resorted to faking
their numbers.

00:10:25.069 --> 00:10:33.670
In the years leading up to 2015, Toshiba had
overstated their operating profits by $1.2

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:34.670
billion.

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:39.009
As you would guess, this was followed up with
the CEO resigning and investor trust being

00:10:39.009 --> 00:10:40.009
crushed.

00:10:40.009 --> 00:10:43.189
Looking forward, Toshiba doesn’t really
have much going for them.

00:10:43.189 --> 00:10:47.431
The majority of their business is just selling
industrial electronics, but it doesn’t seem

00:10:47.431 --> 00:10:49.720
like Toshiba is innovating much there either.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:54.730
So, it may just be a matter of time until
a competitor overtakes this industry either.

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:59.910
Hopefully, Toshiba gets their act together
given that they still employ 117,300 people,

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:04.240
but Toshiba can’t be propped up forever
just to maintain the jobs.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:08.569
Moreover, Toshiba has already reduced their
workforce by 100,000 people over the past

00:11:08.569 --> 00:11:09.569
decade.

00:11:09.569 --> 00:11:13.901
So, if worse comes to worst, it won't be long
till the remaining 100,000 are forced to look

00:11:13.901 --> 00:11:15.949
for new employment as well.

00:11:15.949 --> 00:11:17.910
But that’s just what I think.

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:19.829
Did you guys ever have a Toshiba laptop?

00:11:19.829 --> 00:11:21.149
Comment that down below.

00:11:21.149 --> 00:11:25.490
Also, drop a like if you’re glad to hear
that newer more innovative companies are replacing

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:26.490
such laggards.

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:30.490
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in other languages

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:36.539
